[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: The site’s edge-router firewall (iptables) on router6 is blocking forwarded return traffic. Users are in subnet 172.18.51.0/24 with gateway 172.18.51.254 (router6 eth1).

Mechanism: Hosts in 172.18.51.0/24 can reach the gateway and each other (pc15 -> 172.18.51.254 and -> 172.18.51.16 both succeed), but any traffic to other sites times out after the first hop. Traceroute from pc15 shows hop 1 = 172.18.51.254 and then all stars. From router6 itself, pings to remote hosts succeed, since locally originated traffic bypasses FORWARD filtering. This behavior is classic for a stateful firewall dropping return traffic.

Localization: On router6, iptables shows:
- “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP” (plus specific TCP/UDP DROP rules on eth2).
eth2 is the upstream interface (10.50.1.2 to router3). Thus, all packets returning from upstream networks enter via eth2 and are dropped in FORWARD, preventing replies from ever reaching the LAN. IP forwarding is enabled (cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward = 1) and routing is correct (RIP routes present), so the block is the filter, not routing.

Root cause: A misconfigured iptables policy on router6 that drops all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2, eliminating return paths from other sites.

Corrective fix: On router6, either flush the bad rules (iptables -F FORWARD) or add proper stateful allows before the drops:
- iptables -I FORWARD 1 -m conntrack --ctstate ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
- iptables -A FORWARD -i eth1 -o eth2 -j ACCEPT
Remove the blanket “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP.” This will restore site-wide connectivity without a reboot.
=== END ===